WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.550
Ah, Hollywood.

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:05.790
It's a town where the streets are paved with
gold…and dreams die every single day.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.110
Seriously, where's that Goonies sequel we
were promised?

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.490
And what the heck happened to Inception 2?

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.820
But while plenty of sequels to mega-hit films
have struggled and failed to claw their way

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.310
out of development hell, few follow-ups have
endured more setbacks than Gladiator 2.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:23.550
The long-awaited follow-up to one of Ridley
Scott's most popular movies, Gladiator 2 has

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.509
valiantly tried to battle its way into theaters.

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:29.739
But every time it rises up, the film is beaten
back down.

00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:33.960
It's time to grab your sword and helmet and
finally figure out what happened.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.620
Well, Maximus is dead

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:41.080
When Gladiator hit theaters in 2000, the sword-and-sandal
epic wowed critics — it currently holds

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:47.039
an impressive score on Rotten Tomatoes — and
it took home $457 million at the worldwide

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.039
box office.

00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:52.970
The movie also fared well during awards season,
taking home the Oscar for Best Picture and

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.450
nabbing the Best Actor prize for Russell Crowe.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:01:00.090
The film pretty much turned Crowe into an
international superstar, and nearly two decades

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.800
later, people still shout lines like this:

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.840
"Are you not entertained?"

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.040
Naturally, with such a massive hit on their
hands, DreamWorks was quick to start developing

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.180
a sequel.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.730
There was just one major, Coliseum-sized problem
with the idea: Maximus Decimus Meridius, the

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.730
main character of the movie, is kinda, y'know,
dead.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:24.360
In the film's climactic moments, the evil
Emperor Commodus, played by Joaquin Phoenix,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.890
challenges Maximus to a gladiator battle.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:31.110
There's no universe where Joaquin Phoenix
beats Russell Crowe in hand-to-hand combat,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:36.280
so Commodus evens the odds by secretly stabbing
Maximus before tossing him into the arena.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.750
Luckily, Maximus is fueled by revenge and
righteous anger.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.140
Fighting through the pain, Maximus kills Commodus
with the emperor's own weapon before drifting

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.190
away into the wheat-filled afterlife.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.690
It was a killer ending for a standalone film.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.420
But it certainly makes things tricky for a
sequel.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.320
But hey, Hollywood had gotten around this
kind of problem before, right?

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.320
Prequel problems

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.151
These days, prequels are all the rage, with
everything from Star Wars, to X-Men, and the

00:02:01.151 --> 00:02:04.040
Alien franchise going back to move forward.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.780
DreamWorks was looking to do the same thing
to get Maximus back into the world of the

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:08.780
living.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.030
When people started whispering about a new
Gladiator movie in 2001, the word on the web

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:17.030
was that the story would take place years
before the events of the first film.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.520
It was said to focus on Maximus' early days
in the Roman military, showing the Spaniard's

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.500
rise from mere soldier to mighty general.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.290
Producer Douglas Wick declared talks were
underway to get the ball rolling, saying:

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.220
”You don't finally do something like [Gladiator
2] unless you have a dazzling story to tell."

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:38.370
The studio was reportedly looking to pay Crowe
as much as $20 million to head back into the

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:39.370
arena.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.920
The studio lined up David Franzoni, one of
the original movie's writers, to put the script

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.920
together.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.310
But, much like a Roman mob, Hollywood can
be fickle —

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.109
“Maximus the Merciful!”

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:53.310
- and the project never made it out of development.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.540
Shifting to a sequel

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.650
Sometime between 2001 and 2002, DreamWorks
decided to put the brakes on a Gladiator prequel

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.930
and shift their focus to a sequel.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:08.280
The first big change involved David Franzoni
stepping away from his typewriter to produce.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.880
The writing gig then went to John Logan, another
one of the original Gladiator writers.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.400
With a new guy putting words on the page,
the story was suddenly set 15 years after

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.310
the events of the original film.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:24.040
That same year, producer Walter Parkes hinted
the film might possibly jump back and forth

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.650
in time, but it would mainly tell the story
of Lucius, the royal son of Lucilla.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.510
It would’ve followed Lucius as the last
heir of Marcus Aurelius as he sought out his

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.209
true father — who, we would have discovered,
would turn out to be Maximus.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:41.790
Director Ridley Scott seemed on board with
the idea, even declaring that Logan's script

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.710
was ready to go at one point.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:48.030
However, the sequel was going to be significantly
different from the first film when it came

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.069
to action.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:53.880
As Scott explained, he wasn't going to "touch
gladiators again" — a potentially controversial

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.190
choice for a movie tentatively called "Gladiator
2."

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.590
Instead, Scott was more interested in the
political arena and wanted to focus on "politics

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.250
and praetorians" rather than blood and guts.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:10.849
Scott also expected to start filming in 2005,
but as 2004 faded into memory, Gladiator 2

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:12.720
was still stuck in the cinematic mud.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.840
But, work continued, and the film eventually
morphed into one of the greatest movies never

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.840
made.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.499
Nick Cave's Christ Killer

00:04:20.499 --> 00:04:24.569
If you've ever heard a Nick Cave song, then
you know the Australian artist is basically

00:04:24.569 --> 00:04:26.979
an old-timey prophet armed with a guitar.

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:32.060
His songs are brutal, apocalyptic, and spiritual
in an Old Testament sort of way, and you get

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:35.340
that same vibe watching the movies he's been
involved with over the years.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.879
Cave has composed music for films like Hell
or High Water and The Assassination of Jesse

00:04:40.879 --> 00:04:43.069
James by the Coward Robert Ford.

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:46.810
He's also written scripts for movies like
Lawless and The Proposition.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.759
With that kind of cinematic mind, it's not
surprising that Russell Crowe saw potential

00:04:50.759 --> 00:04:55.330
in Cave's fire-and-brimstone brain, asking
Cave to write the screenplay for Gladiator

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:56.330
2.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.129
What is a bit surprising is how absolutely
insane the script turned out to be.

00:05:00.129 --> 00:05:04.210
Cave didn't disappoint when it came to figuring
out how to follow-up Maximus’ death.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.810
The project was titled "Christ Killer," and
would’ve opened in the afterlife, with Maximus

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.430
realizing he's dead.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.330
Unfortunately, there's nary a wheat field
in sight, and his wife and child are nowhere

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.330
to be seen.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.910
Eventually, the gladiator comes face-to-face
with the dying gods.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:23.660
The decrepit deities make a deal with Maximus,
promising to guide him to his family if he'll

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:27.840
kill a god who's gone rogue — one who's
betrayed the Roman faith and now professes

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.729
belief in the "one, true God."

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:34.129
As Maximus agrees to do their dirty work,
he eventually winds up on Earth, several years

00:05:34.129 --> 00:05:35.229
in the future.

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:39.639
After returning to the real world, he discovers
little Lucius has grown up to be a savage

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:43.659
soldier, one with a bad habit of violently
murdering Christians.

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:47.789
Maximus eventually takes a stand with the
persecuted Christians, leading an army against

00:05:47.789 --> 00:05:52.840
the Roman legions, but after his victory,
he's cursed to go through time as an immortal,

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.050
fighting in countless wars and popping up
in every conflict from the Crusades to Vietnam.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:01.640
If you think that sounds wild, we didn't even
mention the purgatory scene, the Coliseum

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.599
filled with alligators, or the finale that
takes place inside the Pentagon.

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:07.809
Yeah, the one in Washington, D.C.

00:06:07.809 --> 00:06:11.009
Cave described the script as "a stone-cold
masterpiece."

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:12.009
Sure.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:13.009
Why not?

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:14.009
Not over yet

00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:18.360
It’s been pretty quiet the past decade,
but Ridley Scott still hasn’t given up on

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.360
Gladiator.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.930
In March of 2017, Scott said he does "know
how to bring him back," in reference to Maximus.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.860
So, never give up, right?

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.580
Maximus wouldn't.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:28.979
“UNGH!”

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.180
Thanks for watching!

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.119
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:36.119 --> 00:06:38.479
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

